---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Overtime Pay Guides | overtimechecker.com';
const description = 'Plain-English guides on overtime pay, FLSA exemptions, state overtime laws, and what to do when your employer won\'t pay — reviewed by wage-law specialists.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Overtime Pay Guides</h1>
  <p>Practical guides on overtime law for employees and employers. Written by The Click Lab's editorial team, reviewed for accuracy against federal FLSA regulations and state wage statutes. Reviewed by the <a href="/editorial">editorial team</a>.</p>

  <h2>Understanding overtime</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/guides/how-overtime-is-calculated">How overtime is calculated</a></strong><br />
      The math behind overtime pay under the FLSA and state laws. Covers the federal 1.5× rule for hours over 40/week, how the regular rate of pay is calculated (including bonuses and shift differentials that must be included), daily overtime in California (1.5× over 8 hours, 2× over 12), and worked examples for hourly and salaried non-exempt employees. Also covers why some payments are excluded from the regular rate and how to spot calculation errors in your paycheck.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/guides/state-overtime-laws-explained">State overtime laws explained</a></strong><br />
      Federal FLSA sets the minimum — but 20+ states have their own overtime rules that can be more favorable for workers. Covers California's daily overtime (the most complex state regime), Alaska and Nevada daily overtime, Colorado's requirements, and how state and federal rules interact when both apply to the same worker. Includes the critical rule: whichever law gives the employee more protection governs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/guides/common-overtime-misconceptions">Common overtime misconceptions</a></strong><br />
      The most frequent wrong beliefs about overtime — including "salaried employees don't get overtime" (wrong for most workers below the salary threshold), "comp time is legal for private employers" (wrong under federal law), "managers are always exempt" (wrong — job title doesn't determine exemption), and "I agreed to work without overtime" (unenforceable under the FLSA). If your employer has told you something that sounds like it excuses them from paying overtime, read this first.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Practical action</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/guides/what-to-do-if-your-employer-wont-pay-overtime">What to do if your employer won't pay overtime</a></strong><br />
      Step-by-step recovery guide when overtime is being withheld. Covers calculating exactly what you're owed (with records to preserve), sending a written demand, filing a free wage claim with the US Department of Labor Wage and Hour Division or your state labor agency, consulting an employment attorney (most take overtime cases on contingency), and the FLSA's 3-year lookback for willful violations. Includes what NOT to do — informal agreements to waive overtime (unenforceable), retaliation fears (anti-retaliation provisions explained), and waiting too long (statute of limitations issues).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related calculators</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/">Overtime pay calculator</a> — estimate unpaid overtime under FLSA and state rules</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
